    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Objectively:** **Yes, it is a strong, above-median initial offer for Google L6 (Senior/Staff PM)**. \n\n* **Base Salary ($235,000):** Sits near the 65th percentile of Google's posted $183,000\u2013$271,000 SF band.\n* **Target Bonus (20% / $47,000):** Standard and non-negotiable for L6.\n* **RSUs ($1,080,000 / 4 yrs):** Above the median initial grant for L6 (~$800k\u2013$950k).\n\n**Subjectively (as a happy Meta IC6):** **It is a lateral move financially, not a clear upgrade.** \n\nBecause you did not seek this role out and are already well-positioned at Meta, **you should negotiate.** Google rarely leads with their best offer, and initial offers typically exclude sign-on bonuses specifically to leave room for negotiation.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Understanding the Real Numbers: The Vesting Cliff\n\nGoogle does not vest equity evenly at 25% per year. Google uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (most commonly **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**):\n\n| Component | Year 1 (33% Vest) | Year 2 (33% Vest) | Year 3 (22% Vest) | Year 4 (12% Vest) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base** | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Bonus (20%)** | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 |\n| **Equity Vest** | $356,400 | $356,400 | $237,600 | $129,600 |\n| **Total Comp (TC)** | **~$638,400** | **~$638,400** | **~$519,600** | **~$411,600** |\n\n*(Note: On a flat 4-year average, annualized TC is **~$552,000/year**).*\n\n#### Why this matters:\n* **Years 1 & 2** look competitive against Meta IC6 total comp.\n* **Years 3 & 4** experience a severe drop-off (the \"Google Cliff\"). While annual refreshers will stack, the recruiter is correct: refreshers depend on performance ratings and company budgets. At Meta, your quarterly refreshers are likely already stacking steadily on top of an even 25%/year vest.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Negotiate and Exact Targets\n\nYou hold maximum leverage: **you are currently employed at their primary competitor, happy in your role, and they came to you.**\n\nUse the following counter-targets:\n\n| Lever | Current Offer | Your Counter Target | Realistic Landing Target |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000** | $40,000 \u2013 $60,000 |\n| **RSUs (4-year)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n\n#### Why these levers:\n1. **Sign-on Bonus:** Google's Compensation Committee views sign-on bonuses as a separate budget pool. Leaving Meta means walking away from unvested equity and imminent bonus cycles\u2014making an initial $0 sign-on standard procedure for Google to test if you'll accept without pushing back.\n2. **Equity (RSUs):** Google has substantial room to stretch equity at L6. Pushing to ~$1.2M\u2013$1.25M cushions the Year 3/4 drop-off and brings Year 1 TC closer to ~$700k+.\n3. **Base Salary:** Pushing into the mid-$250s stays within their approved $271k band limit and elevates your permanent floor.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nWhen speaking with the recruiter, remain collaborative, enthusiastic about the team, and firm on the economic reality:\n\n> *\"I\u2019ve really enjoyed getting to know the team and the problem space; I\u2019m very excited about what we could build together. That said, as we discussed early on, I\u2019m in a strong position at Meta with solid momentum, upcoming refreshers, and substantial unvested equity.*\n> \n> *Looking at the numbers and accounting for Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule, the current offer is essentially a lateral move over a 4-year horizon, especially in Years 3 and 4.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy decision for me to walk away from my current trajectory at Meta, I would need to see:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000***\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,250,000***\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$80,000** to bridge the transition and forfeited Meta incentives.*\n> \n> *If we can get the package to that level, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n### Recommendation Summary\n* **Do not accept as-is.**\n* **Negotiate immediately:** Counter for higher equity, an improved base, and a sign-on bonus.\n* **Walk away only if** they refuse to budge entirely and you value your current Meta trajectory and stacked refreshers more than the Google team/product opportunity.",
